import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

public class Solution {

    /**
     * Calculates (g^n) % p using the binary exponentiation algorithm.
     * This method is efficient for large exponents, operating in O(log n) time complexity.
     * 
     * @param g The base value.
     * @param n The exponent value.
     * @param p The modulus.
     * @return The result of (g^n) % p.
     */
    public int modp(int g, int n, int p) {
        long result = 1;
        long base = g % p;
        long exponent = n;

        while (exponent > 0) {
            if ((exponent % 2) == 1) {
                result = (result * base) % p;
            }
            base = (base * base) % p;
            exponent = exponent / 2;
        }
        return (int) result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution sol = new Solution();
        System.out.println(sol.modp(5, 4, 497));
        System.out.println(sol.modp(7, 3, 517));
        System.out.println(sol.modp(6, 1, 311));
        System.out.println(sol.modp(2, 10, 101));
        System.out.println(sol.modp(10, 100, 701));
    }
}

The Diffie-Hellman key exchange is a foundational protocol in modern computer science and network security. It enables two parties who have no prior knowledge of each other to jointly establish a shared secret key over an insecure communications channel. This shared secret can subsequently be used to encrypt all subsequent communications using a symmetric-key cipher. The mathematical beauty of this protocol lies in the difficulty of the discrete logarithm problem. 

* The modular exponentiation function implemented above, often called "modp," serves as the core computational engine for the Diffie-Hellman key exchange process.
* To perform this operation efficiently, we employ the algorithm known as exponentiation by squaring or the binary exponentiation method.
* This method is essential because calculating (g^n) directly for very large values of n would result in numbers far too vast for standard computer memory to hold, and it would be computationally infeasible.
* By using the property (a * b) % p = ((a % p) * (b % p)) % p, we ensure that the intermediate results never exceed the capacity of a long integer type in Java, provided the modulus p is within a reasonable range.
* The algorithm functions by breaking the exponent n into its binary representation, effectively processing each bit to determine whether to include the current base in the final product.
* During the loop, we square the base in each iteration, which allows the algorithm to converge upon the final result in logarithmic time, making it highly scalable for cryptographic applications.
* It is critical in production environments to be wary of potential side-channel attacks when implementing modular exponentiation, though the standard implementation provided is the functional baseline.
* The security of the protocol relies on the fact that while exponentiation is computationally cheap, the inverse operation, finding the exponent given the result, is effectively impossible for sufficiently large prime numbers.
* Developers utilizing this code should ensure that the modulus p is a large prime number to guarantee the security properties required for real-world cryptographic systems.
* Always consider the use of BigInteger in Java if the inputs or the modulus exceed the 32-bit integer limit, as overflow will lead to incorrect calculations and potential security vulnerabilities.

